<template name="tus_settings_dspaudio_Monomachine">
<head>
</head>
<body>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<h1>Render Ahead</h1>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<div id="btRenderAhead0" class="settings-checkboxwithlabel">
		<button id="button" class="settings-checkbox"/>
		<label>Off (lowest latency)</label>
	</div>
	<div id="btRenderAhead1" class="settings-checkboxwithlabel">
		<button id="button" class="settings-checkbox"/>
		<label>1 block</label>
	</div>
	<div id="btRenderAhead2" class="settings-checkboxwithlabel">
		<button id="button" class="settings-checkbox"/>
		<label>2 blocks</label>
	</div>
	<div id="btRenderAhead4" class="settings-checkboxwithlabel">
		<button id="button" class="settings-checkbox"/>
		<label>4 blocks</label>
	</div>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label>Off runs the machine inside your DAW's audio callback: the lowest latency, but a moment of high CPU load can click. With render ahead the machine runs that many blocks ahead on a thread of its own, so a slow moment is absorbed instead of heard. Your DAW compensates the added latency for playback and recording; you notice it only when playing live. Try 1 block first, 2 if it still clicks. Saved as the default for new instances.</label>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label>Latency (blocks) above is a different setting: it only delays incoming MIDI.</label>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<h1>Audio Input A/B</h1>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<label id="audioInputStatus"/>
	<settingsspacer1/>
	<button id="btAudioInputSettings" class="button">Open Audio/MIDI Settings...</button>
	<settingsspacer1/>
</body>
</template>
